---
name: agents-update
description: Reconcile AGENTS.md with what the code and docs now do, then keep CLAUDE.md a thin pointer to it. Use after a batch of code or documentation changes, when AGENTS.md looks stale, or when the user asks to sync, refresh, or update agent instructions to match the codebase.
---

# Agents Update

Code changes; the agent docs do not. That gap is **drift** — AGENTS.md still
describing a command, path, or convention the code has moved past. This skill
reads what actually changed, closes the drift in AGENTS.md, and points CLAUDE.md
at it.

Architecture this skill maintains:

- **AGENTS.md is the single source of truth** — all project guidance lives here.
- **CLAUDE.md is a pointer** — it imports AGENTS.md (`@AGENTS.md`) and holds only
  genuinely Claude-only lines below that. It never copies AGENTS.md content;
  a copy is just drift waiting to happen.

## Steps

### 1. Define the change window and gather what changed

- Resolve the repo root: `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`.
- Pick the window. Default: everything since AGENTS.md was last touched —
  `git log -1 --format=%H -- AGENTS.md` gives that commit; diff it to `HEAD` and
  add uncommitted working-tree changes. If the user names a ref or range, use it.
- Read the changed code AND the changed docs (README, `docs/`, CHANGELOG, ADRs,
  package manifests). Docs and manifests state the *intent* a raw diff hides —
  the new command, the renamed module, the dropped dependency.

**Done when** you hold an itemized change-list — new/removed/renamed commands,
moved structure, new conventions, changed deps — not just "ran git diff".

### 2. Read the current agent docs

- Read AGENTS.md in full, then CLAUDE.md in full. Note any nested AGENTS.md whose
  subtree the changes touched.
- Note AGENTS.md's existing section structure, headings, and tone so later edits
  match it rather than fight it.

**Done when** you can name every section AGENTS.md currently has.

### 3. Map the drift

Walk the change-list against AGENTS.md. Give every item exactly one verdict:

- **reflected** — AGENTS.md already tells the truth; leave it.
- **drifted** — stale, wrong, or missing; AGENTS.md needs an edit.
- **not-agent-relevant** — a coding agent never needs it; skip by judgment.

Agent-relevant means: build / test / run commands, project structure, naming and
style conventions, gotchas, do-not-touch zones, workflow. Not agent-relevant:
one-off bugfixes, transient task notes, internal changelog detail.

**Done when** every change-list item carries one of the three verdicts — none
left unaccounted for. (A vague pass here is how stale docs survive a "sync".)

### 4. Close the drift in AGENTS.md

- Surgical edits only. Update the drifted section in place; add a missing one
  where an agent would look for it. Smallest diff that tells the truth.
- Document only what the repo verifies — real commands from manifests/scripts,
  real paths. Never invent a command you did not find locally.
- Match existing headings, ordering, and tone. Do not rewrite untouched sections.
  Do not delete guidance for looking old — delete only what the change-list proves
  wrong.

**Done when** every **drifted** item from step 3 is resolved in AGENTS.md.

### 5. Point CLAUDE.md at AGENTS.md

- CLAUDE.md's body should be the import line `@AGENTS.md`, plus any genuinely
  Claude-only guidance below it.
- If CLAUDE.md duplicates AGENTS.md content, that duplication is drift — collapse
  it to the pointer. If CLAUDE.md is missing, create the pointer. If it already
  points correctly, leave it.

**Done when** CLAUDE.md references AGENTS.md via `@AGENTS.md` and holds zero
duplicated AGENTS.md content.

### 6. Verify and report

- Re-read the edited AGENTS.md sections. Confirm each command/path you wrote
  actually exists in the repo. Confirm no duplicate headings.
- Confirm CLAUDE.md resolves to AGENTS.md with no copy drift.
- Report: files changed, the drift items closed, and the items deliberately
  **skipped** as not-agent-relevant — name them, so the skip is visible rather
  than silent.

## Reference

**What belongs in AGENTS.md:** how to build, test, and run; project layout and
entry points; naming/style/commit conventions; environment and tooling setup;
known gotchas and do-not-touch zones; the project's workflow. Keep it to what a
fresh coding agent needs before its first edit.

**CLAUDE.md pointer shape:**

```markdown
@AGENTS.md

<!-- Claude-only notes, if any, go below -->
```

The `@AGENTS.md` import pulls the source of truth in; nothing above it, nothing
duplicated below it.
